#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:63
msgid "The Feature Studio is where we can do the calculations for the design of the parts that we are making in the Part Studio. AIDE Feature Studios have 3 main sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:65
msgid "Tree - where we define the function calls (designers) and input parameters (params) required for the design. In more complex designs the Tree is also used to define the connections with parts that are children or subcomponents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:66
msgid "Designer - where we calculate the dimensions of the part we are designing. The variables that we define are all part of the design map and can be accessed as :code:`design.varName`. Thus the length of a part would be defined as :code:`design.L`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:67
msgid "Feature - this is the code that inserts the results of our calculations (the design map) into the part studio. This feature is inserted as the very first feature in the part studio so that all of the subsequent features have access to the  variables in the design map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:70
msgid "Units in AIDE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:72
msgid "Engineering design calculations should always be done using software tools that support units. Units provide an import check on all calculations and using units ensures that the we don't burn up our spacecraft because of a failure to use the right units in an equation. In Onshape all of our calculations include units. Unfortunately there is a limitation on how we pass parameters from one component to another component as we create complex designs with multiple components. To get around this limitation we have adopted a strict `variable naming convention <https://cad.onshape.com/documents/7e7bfd441e81a4b207bb9b86/w/36fcd65eb9f3ae9f72ccf937/e/9c67513c34ba306ef874295f>`_ so that we know what units a variable has just from looking at its name. Here are a couple examples of variable names, their units [] and an evaluation of whether or not they meet our requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:74
msgid ":code:`myFavorityDimensionIsL` [meter] YES!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:75
msgid ":code:`V_MAX` [oops] NO! subscripts must be lower case - should be :code:`V_max`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:76
msgid ":code:`riverQ` [meter^3/s] YES!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:77
msgid ":code:`riverQm` [L/s] YES! The m really means milli (m^3/s) which is the same as L/s."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:78
msgid ":code:`pipeWallS` [meter] YES! This is the Space between a pipe and a wall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:81
msgid "Hydraulic functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:83
msgid "AguaClara has added a limited number of hydraulic-related functions in FeatureScript in `AguaClara Core FS <https://cad.onshape.com/documents/6750b53736b16374e515f93d/w/e1f0d6546c0ebff38956e2f7/e/397556f7ec0f2b14bcbdc049>`_. The most commonly used functions for this course are in the tabs headloss and physical chemistry. The pipe design equations that work for both laminar and turbulent flow regimes are found in those two tabs. For example the function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:85
msgid ":code:`flowPipe (diam, headloss, length, nu, roughness, kMinor)`  will calculate the flow rate in a given pipe given the available energy (head loss)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:86
msgid "The companion function to calculate the minimum required pipe diameter given a desired flow rate is :code:`diamPipe(flowRate, head loss, length, nu, pipeRough, kMinor)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:87
msgid "The actual head loss given a pipe and a flow rate is :code:`pipeHeadloss(flowRate, diam, length, nu, pipeRough, kMinor)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:88
msgid "The density and viscosity of water are available as functions of temperature in the physical chemistry tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/ET_Design_Challenge.rst:90
msgid "You can access these functions quickly while editing in featurescript by typing the part of the function name that you remember and Onshape will provide a list of possible functions for you to select from."
